T16. a second clock write APPENDS a correction and never replaces

  ok       T16a. CONTROL -- a FIRST clock-in on a visit with none recorded is stored as the clock time itself and raises NO correction entry. Without this arm the whole design could be "every clock write is a pending correction" and nothing would ever be an EVV record
  ok       T16b. a DIFFERENT clock_in_at over a recorded one is ACCEPTED, not refused. This is the half that replaces N1: a caregiver who clocked in at the wrong time on their own visit had no route at all under the 409, because propose_clock_correction is scheduler-only
  ok       T16c. ...and THE ORIGINAL IS STILL THE CLOCK TIME ON THE ROW. An append that also moved the field would be the overwrite wearing a trail -- "a state audit that finds an edited time with no trail cannot tell a typo correction from a fabrication", and a trail beside a silently moved value answers that no better
  ok       T16d. ...and exactly ONE correction entry was appended
  ok       T16e. ...carrying the OLD value and the NEW value. Both numbers, on the record, which is the whole point of an additive correction
  ok       T16f. ...attributed to the actor from the VERIFIED SESSION. A correction attributed from the payload is a correction attributed by whoever is submitting it
  ok       T16g. ...with a reason from the closed vocabulary and its label. A correction with a blank reason is the silent drop with extra steps -- and the caller supplied none here, so the route's own code is used and is NOT left empty
  ok       T16h. ...and a SERVER timestamp for when it was submitted, which is a different fact from the time being proposed
  ok       T16i. ...and it is PENDING. "A scheduler approves it before payroll uses it" is the decision, so an unapproved self-submitted time must not be indistinguishable from an approved one
  ok       T16j. ...and the CALLER IS TOLD at the time -- the submission is pending and needs an approval. A 200 that looks like an accepted clock change is affirmative false success, which is worse than a refusal

T16. a pending correction does not move the effective clock
  ok       T16k. effectiveClock IGNORES a pending entry -- the effective value is still the recorded one. This is the arm that carries the approval requirement into the number payroll reads; without it `pending` is a decoration
  ok       T16l. ...and it SAYS a correction is awaiting approval rather than dropping it from view. A submission nobody can see is a submission nobody will approve
  ok       T16m. ...and an APPROVED entry does move it, with the original still returned beside it
  ok       T16n. CONTROL -- an entry written BEFORE this change, with no `status` at all, is still effective. Treating a missing status as pending would silently revert every live corrected visit to its uncorrected time, which is this change causing the defect it is fixing

T16. the approval route, and who may use it
  ok       T16o. a CAREGIVER cannot approve -- 403 and nothing written. The whole reason the correction is pending is that an unverified self-assertion is what EVV exists to stop; a caregiver who can approve their own submission has the overwrite back with two extra fields
  ok       T16p. a SCHEDULER-tier caller approves it, and the entry becomes approved
  ok       T16q. ...recording WHO approved it and WHEN, from the session and the server. An approval with no approver is the same unattributed act the submission was not allowed to be
  ok       T16r. ...and APPROVAL STILL DOES NOT MOVE THE FIELD. The row's clock_in_at is the caregiver's original forever; what approval moves is which value effectiveClock reports
  ok       T16s. re-approving is answered deliberately rather than silently appending a second approval. Either is defensible; what is not is a second approval record for one decision
  ok       T16t. an index that is not there is refused 400 with nothing written, not quietly ignored with a 200

T16 controls -- what must NOT have moved
  ok       T16u. CONTROL -- an IDENTICAL re-send appends NOTHING. sairnsenior queues clock events offline and replays them UNCHANGED, so the same visit legitimately arrives twice; turning every replay into a pending correction would bury the real ones
  ok       T16v. CONTROL -- the OTHER half of the visit still writes normally. The rule is per field and a clock-out after a clock-in is the ordinary case, not a correction
  ok       T16w. clock_out_at is covered by the same rule, not just clock_in_at. A rule proven on one of two fields covers one of two fields
  ok       T16x. CONTROL -- a SCHEDULER-tier write still cannot touch an EVV field, and does not get a correction entry as a side door either. Their route is propose_clock_correction, which this change leaves alone
  ok       T16y. CONTROL -- a time AHEAD of the server that received it is still refused 400 with nothing written. The append path must not become a route around checkClockEvent
  ok       T16z. a reason the caller DID supply is the one recorded. The route's own code covers the silent case and does not overwrite a real one

